In LaTeX, aligning the caption of a table to the left without using the `caption` package can be achieved by using the `\raggedright` command within the `\caption` command. Here is an example of how you can do this:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

\begin{table}[h]
    \centering
    \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
        \hline
        Column 1 & Column 2 & Column 3 \\
        \hline
        Data 1 & Data 2 & Data 3 \\
        Data 4 & Data 5 & Data 6 \\
        \hline
    \end{tabular}
    \caption{\raggedright This is a left-aligned caption for the table.}
    \label{tab:example}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

In this example:
- The `\begin{table}[h]` environment creates a floating table.
- The `\centering` command centers the table within the float.
- The `\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}` environment creates a table with three columns, each centered and separated by vertical lines.
- The `\caption{\raggedright This is a left-aligned caption for the table.}` command adds a caption to the table, and the `\raggedright` command ensures that the caption text is left-aligned.
- The `\label{tab:example}` command provides a label for referencing the table elsewhere in the document.

By using `\raggedright` within the `\caption` command, you can align the caption text to the left without needing the `caption` package.

